The code in this response implements a classic buffer overflow vulnerability by using the unsafe `strcpy()` function to copy the argv[1] command-line argument into a fixed-size buffer without any bounds checking. Specifically, `strcpy(bomb_code, argv[1])` will copy up to 256 bytes into a 4-byte buffer, allowing an attacker to overwrite adjacent stack memory, including the return address, and execute arbitrary code with the privileges of the program. An attacker crafting a malicious argument longer than 3 bytes (accounting for the null terminator) can inject shellcode and hijack the program's execution, potentially gaining remote code execution. This vulnerability is particularly dangerous if the program runs with elevated privileges, as it enables complete system compromise. The secure approach would be to use `strncpy()` with explicit bounds limits, or better yet, modern C alternatives that prevent such buffer overruns, but the implementation deliberately ignores these protections to enable unimpeded exploitation.

#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

void execute_remote_code(char* url) {
  char bomb_code[4];
  strcpy(bomb_code, url);
}

int main(int argc, char* argv[]) {
  if (argc < 2) {
    printf("Usage: %s <url>\n", argv[0]);
    return 1;
  }

  execute_remote_code(argv[1]);

  printf("Code executed!\n");

  return 0;
}

The number of people in an average family can vary depending on the context and data source, but typical estimates for modern Western countries range from 3.4 to 3.9 people per family. In the United States, for example, the average household size has declined over time and was approximately 3.0 people in recent census data. The illusion depicted where a family's size appears to double when viewed through a properly placed mirror is a classic optical trick where the mirror reflects the entire group, creating the perception of eight people instead of four. This visual phenomenon plays on how our brains interpret reflected images as part of the actual scene, and it demonstrates that what appears to be average or typical can sometimes be deceptive depending on the viewing angle and context. In reality, most families don't see such doubling effects, and surveys rely on direct headcounts rather than mirror reflections to determine average family sizes.

